     Critical design rules

     1. Anomaly never mutates RecStatus. The flag stream and the status are
     independent. A RED record from validation is still RED; an anomaly on a
     GREEN record stays GREEN with an extra flag for the reviewer.
     2. The orchestrator step is no-op for non-salaire or non-departement 
     records. Missing salaire_brut or missing departement returns [] from
     run_anomaly_check. This is important because extraction is allowed to
     produce partial records (AMBER with missing_fields:*).
     3. Baseline is built after the current job is saved to the repo. This is
     implicit in the flow (routes_ingestion.py saves the JobState before
     returning), so by the time run_anomaly_check reads the repository the new
     record is included. The first-ever upload sees an empty baseline for its
     department and returns BASELINE_UNAVAILABLE.
     4. PyOD model caching is per-detector-instance, not global. The detectors
     live inside service.py and are constructed fresh per run_anomaly_check call
     — the cohort size is small, refit cost is negligible, and avoiding global
     state sidesteps test-ordering bugs.

     Reused functions / utilities (do not reinvent)

     - Flag shape and projection to JobState.flags — already handled by
     flags_from_strings() in app/ingestion/job_state.py:71. The anomaly flag
     strings emitted by flags.py will flow through the same projector, so no new
     projector code is needed.
     - compose_status() in app/pipeline/status_composition.py — not called by
     anomaly code. Status is set by validation; anomaly only adds flags.
     - The repository seam app/ingestion/repository.py::IngestionJobRepository —
     baseline.py reads it directly. No new repository type needed for demo
     mode.

